ly, native shamanist beliefs, Bon ideology, and imported Buddhist concepts." 15 When he turns to a discussion of "Lamaist imagery," we find wrathful deities (most of which were in fact derived from India) represented as manifestations of the Tibetan character. Pal discusses the "imagery of the terrifying deities" in Tibet's repressive society: Nothing is more characteristic of the Tibetan psyche than their love for the grotesque and the bizarre in their art. Terrifying gods and wrathful demons, malevolent spirits and grinning skeletons prance and dance about on the surface of tankas ... in an orgiastic exhibition of their strength and power.

